 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by rasterization $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ and backward pass. SH degree increase adds $O(N \cdot d_{SH}^2)$ to gradient computation.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot d_{SH}^2)$ for SH coefficients. Bilateral grid adds $O(H \cdot W \cdot C_{grid})$.
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling:** Scales linearly with number of Gaussians $N$ and image resolution $H \cdot W$.
-- **Strengths:** Explicit SH degree progression balances quality and speed. Visibility-aware Adam reduces memory and computation for sparse scenes. Bilateral grid enhances view-dependent effects.
-- **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of regularization weights. Depth loss requires accurate ground truth depth.
-- **When to Use:** Standard 3D Gaussian Splatting training with optional depth and appearance enhancements.
-- **When Not to Use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and SH degree progression is too costly.
+- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by rasterization $O(N \cdot P)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $P$ is the number of pixels. The bilateral grid slicing adds overhead proportional to the grid resolution.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for Gaussian parameters. Bilateral grids add $O(G)$ where $G$ is the grid size.
+- **Strengths:** Explicit depth supervision improves geometric consistency. Bilateral grids allow for high-frequency appearance details without increasing Gaussian count. Progressive SH degree prevents early-stage instability.
+- **Limitations:** Depth loss requires accurate ground truth depth. Bilateral grids introduce additional hyperparameters and potential artifacts if not regularized.
+- **When to use:** When high-fidelity appearance and geometric consistency are required, and ground truth depth is available.
 
